entry.summary:
The rule I'd remember





  Optimistic locking is particularly good when:



multiple actors can read and later modify the same entity;
there can be significant time between read and write;
concurrent modifications are possible but not normally frequent;
silently overwriting somebody else's change would be incorrect.
It becomes less attractive when conflicts are expected constantly.

Out of the box, vx folds the whole lockfile into the workspace pnpm install that pnpm-lock.yaml invalidates every cached task, and --affected It is also the single largest source of "why did everything rebuild" @vzn/vx-lockfile ships one plugin per package manager: pnpm(), bun(), npm(), yarn(). Each claims its lockfile out of the fingerprint seam and instead its project's resolved dependency :
// vx.workspace.ts import { defineWorkspace } from '@vzn/vx/config' import { pnpm } from '@vzn/vx-lockfile'
export default defineWorkspace({ plugins: [pnpm()] })
Nothing else changes. vx why names the material as plugin @vzn/vx-lockfile/pnpm, and the workspace fingerprint line The closure is exactly what the project's node_modules can resolve: its dependencies, devDependencies and optionalDepend…
